SUMMARY:Diplomacy Lab: Learn How to Serve as a Consultant for the U.S. De
 partment of State (Spring 2027 Recruitment)
DESCRIPTION:The Diplomacy Lab is a practitioner-led partnership that allo
 ws the Hamilton Lugar School to provide direct analysis for the U.S. Depa
 rtment of State. This isn't just a research project—it's a chance to co
 nsult on urgent global challenges\, from climate security to digital free
 dom\, directly from Bloomington.  Join us on September 11 for a strategic
  briefing on the Spring 2027 cycle. We will move beyond the syllabus to s
 how you how to form a team\, align with faculty expertise\, and submit a 
 winning bid.What we will cover:The Matchmaking Strategy: Using our intern
 al interest lists to pair student technical skills with faculty subject-m
 atter expertise.    The Bidding Mechanics: A step-by-step guide to the pr
 oject menu and the requirements for faculty-led submissions.    The 3-Cre
 dit Standard: Why we are standardizing the Lab experience to ensure profe
 ssional parity and academic rigor.    Lessons Learned: A panel of HLS fac
 ulty and students will discuss their experiences managing previous teams 
 and project IDs.    Who should attend?Faculty looking to integrate practi
 tioner-led tradecraft into their research.    Students eager to build a p
 rofessional portfolio of "Day 1" assets.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p data-path-to-node="6">  The <strong data-
 path-to-node="6" data-index-in-node="10">Diplomacy Lab</strong> is a prac
 titioner-led partnership that allows the Hamilton Lugar School to provide
  direct analysis for the U.S. Department of State. This isn't just a rese
 arch project—it's a chance to consult on urgent global challenges\, fro
 m climate security to digital freedom\, directly from Bloomington.</p><p 
 data-path-to-node="7">  Join us on September 11 for a strategic briefing 
 on the Spring 2027 cycle. We will move beyond the syllabus to show you ho
 w to form a team\, align with faculty expertise\, and submit a winning bi
 d.</p><p data-path-to-node="8"><strong data-path-to-node="8" data-index-i
 n-node="0">What we will cover:</strong></p><ul data-path-to-node="9"><li>
 <p data-path-to-node="9\,0\,0"><strong data-path-to-node="9\,0\,0" data-i
 ndex-in-node="0">The Matchmaking Strategy:</strong> Using our internal in
 terest lists to pair student technical skills with faculty subject-matter
  expertise.    </p></li><li><p data-path-to-node="9\,1\,0"><strong data-p
 ath-to-node="9\,1\,0" data-index-in-node="0">The Bidding Mechanics:</stro
 ng> A step-by-step guide to the project menu and the requirements for fac
 ulty-led submissions.    </p></li><li><p data-path-to-node="9\,2\,0"><str
 ong data-path-to-node="9\,2\,0" data-index-in-node="0">The 3-Credit Stand
 ard:</strong> Why we are standardizing the Lab experience to ensure profe
 ssional parity and academic rigor.    </p></li><li><p data-path-to-node="
 9\,3\,0"><strong data-path-to-node="9\,3\,0" data-index-in-node="0">Lesso
 ns Learned:</strong> A panel of HLS faculty and students will discuss the
 ir experiences managing previous teams and project IDs.    </p></li></ul>
 <p data-path-to-node="10"><strong data-path-to-node="10" data-index-in-no
 de="0">Who should attend?</strong></p><ul data-path-to-node="11"><li><p d
 ata-path-to-node="11\,0\,0"><strong data-path-to-node="11\,0\,0" data-ind
 ex-in-node="0">Faculty</strong> looking to integrate practitioner-led tra
 decraft into their research.    </p></li><li><p data-path-to-node="11\,1\
 ,0"><strong data-path-to-node="11\,1\,0" data-index-in-node="0">Students<
 /strong> eager to build a professional portfolio of "Day 1" assets.    </
 p></li></ul>
